<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lucrezia Borgia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1480">1480</a>–<a href="/years/1519">1519</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-borgia">House of Borgia</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lucrezia Borgia was the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-vi">Pope Alexander VI</a> and sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-borgia">Cesare Borgia</a>. Her first marriage, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-sforza">Giovanni Sforza</a>, lord of Pesaro, was annulled in 1497, and her second husband, Alfonso of Aragon, was murdered in 1500. In 1502 she married Alfonso I d'Este, later Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>, where she presided over a court of poets and artists and was regent during his absences.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lucrezia-borgia.jpg" alt="Lucrezia Borgia" width="320" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Dosso Dossi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dosso_DOSSI_,_Battista_DOSSI_(attributed_to)_-_Lucrezia_Borgia,_Duchess_of_Ferrara_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lucrezia Borgia was born on 18 April 1480 at Subiaco, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, the illegitimate daughter of Cardinal Rodrigo de Borgia, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-vi">Pope Alexander VI</a>, and his mistress Vannozza dei Cattanei. Her father acknowledged her, and she had three full siblings: Cesare, Giovanni and Gioffre. She received a humanist education from intellectuals at court rather than in a convent. She was fluent in Spanish, Valenciano, Italian and French, literate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Greek, proficient in poetry and oration, and could play the lute and zither.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When Rodrigo became pope in August 1492 he called off Lucrezia's earlier engagements and arranged her marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-sforza">Giovanni Sforza</a>, Lord of Pesaro, with a proxy wedding in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on 12 June 1493, when she was 13. When the Borgias no longer needed the Sforzas, the pope sought an annulment. Giovanni at first refused and spread the rumour that the pope wanted his daughter back in Rome for himself, a rumour that endured for centuries and tainted her reputation. He finally signed a confession of impotence, and the annulment was finalised in December 1497.</p><p>On 21 July 1498 she married Alfonso of Aragon, Duke of Bisceglie, an illegitimate son of Alfonso II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. In 1499 she became governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoleto</a>, a post usually reserved to cardinals, the first woman to hold such a governorship in the Papal States. Alfonso was attacked on the steps of St. Peter's on 15 July 1500 and strangled in his bed while recovering. It was widely rumoured that Cesare was responsible, and tradition has it that he had Alfonso killed after his political value waned. Lucrezia then acted as her father's private secretary, and a Venetian ambassador called her wise, discreet and generous.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Lucrezia married Alfonso I d'Este by proxy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on 30 December 1501 and in person at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a> on 2 February 1502. She became Duchess of Ferrara in 1505. Her court became a centre for writers, musicians, poets and philosophers, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludovico-ariosto">Ludovico Ariosto</a>, and she ruled as regent twice while her husband was on campaign. She bought marshland cheaply and invested in its reclamation, nearly doubling her annual income. She bore six children in the marriage.</p><p>She died on 24 June 1519 at Belriguardo Castle, aged 39, after giving birth to a daughter, and was buried at the convent of Corpus Domini in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>. Rumours of incest, poisoning and murder have persisted, among them the tale of a hollow ring used to poison drinks, but no historical basis for them exists beyond the attacks of her enemies.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lucrezia Borgia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His reign became notorious for nepotism and for advancing his children, among them Cesare and <b>Lucrezia Borgia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-11">11 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Alfonso I, husband of <b>Lucrezia Borgia</b>, was famous for his artillery.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-ferrara">Duchy of Ferrara</a></li><li><blockquote>Giovanni Sforza, Costanzo's son, married <b>Lucrezia Borgia</b>, but the marriage was annulled and Cesare Borgia expelled him in 1500.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-pesaro">Lordship of Pesaro</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Subiaco, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.93,13.1&amp;year=1505" aria-label="Subiaco on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4869 671 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Lucrezia Borgia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5319 941h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5319 941h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.93,13.1&amp;year=1505">What was here in 1505? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
